A `div` with a click handler only works with a mouse. It isn't in the tab order, so keyboard users can't reach it; it doesn't react to Enter or Space; and screen readers announce it as plain text, not as a button. Use a real button:

```html
<button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" onclick="saveDraft()">Save draft</button>
```

`type="button"` matters if it ends up inside a `<form>`, where the default type is `submit`. If your `.btn` styles were written for a `div`, the button may pick up browser defaults; resetting them takes a few lines:

```css
.btn {
  font: inherit;
  border: 0;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

Adding `role="button"`, `tabindex="0"` and a keydown handler to the `div` can technically fix it, but you'd be rebuilding by hand what `<button>` gives you for free, and it's easy to miss details like Space activation or the disabled state.

Don't: anyone who can guess or read a user ID can build that token, so it resets any account. The token must be random, single-use, short-lived, and stored only as a hash.

```js
const crypto = require("node:crypto");

// POST /forgot-password
const token = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString("hex");
const tokenHash = crypto.createHash("sha256").update(token).digest("hex");
await db.query(
  `INSERT INTO password_resets (user_id, token_hash, expires_at)
   VALUES ($1, $2, now() + interval '1 hour')`,
  [user.id, tokenHash]
);
// email a link: https://app.example.com/reset?token=<token>
```

When the link is used, hash the received token, look up a row with that hash and `expires_at > now()`, set the new password (bcrypt), then delete that row, plus any other reset tokens for the user.

A fast hash like SHA-256 is fine here, unlike for passwords, because the token has 256 bits of randomness and can't be guessed. Storing only the hash means a leaked database doesn't hand out working reset links.

Also: reply "if that address exists, we've sent an email" whether or not the account exists, and invalidate the user's existing sessions after a successful reset.

Quasi sempre perché il risultato non gli dà quello che cerca, e lui riprova. Apri la trascrizione di uno di questi casi e guarda cosa riceve davvero. Le cause tipiche sono tre:

- il file è lungo e il tool lo tronca, quindi la parte che cerca non arriva mai (serve `offset`/`limit` o un `grep` per trovare la riga);
- il risultato è un errore poco chiaro, per esempio un percorso sbagliato senza dire quale file esiste;
- il contesto è pieno e il risultato precedente è stato tagliato, quindi il modello "non lo vede più".

