Il blocco scuro è ripetuto due volte: è il prezzo per non usare JavaScript nella logica dei colori. Quando cambi un valore scuro, cambialo in entrambi.

Per ricordare la scelta ed evitare il lampo di tema sbagliato al caricamento, metti questo nel `<head>` prima del CSS:

```html
<script>
  try {
    const tema = localStorage.getItem("tema");
    if (tema) document.documentElement.dataset.theme = tema;
  } catch {}
</script>
```

E questa funzione per il bottone (`null` torna a seguire il sistema):

```js
function impostaTema(tema) {
  const root = document.documentElement;
  if (tema) root.dataset.theme = tema;
  else delete root.dataset.theme;
  try {
    if (tema) localStorage.setItem("tema", tema);
    else localStorage.removeItem("tema");
  } catch {}
}
```

Nota `--on-brand`: nel tema scuro il blu è più chiaro, e il testo bianco sopra non avrebbe più contrasto sufficiente, quindi lì i bottoni usano testo scuro. I valori che ho messo superano 4,5:1 in entrambi i temi.

A `<canvas>` is a bitmap to assistive technology, so screen readers announce nothing useful. The fix has two parts: give each canvas a short text alternative, and make the underlying numbers available as real HTML, because a sentence cannot carry a whole dataset.

```html
<figure>
  <figcaption id="signups-title">Weekly sign-ups, last 6 weeks</figcaption>
  <canvas id="signups" role="img" aria-labelledby="signups-title" aria-describedby="signups-summary"></canvas>
  <p id="signups-summary">Sign-ups rose from 120 to 310, with the biggest jump in week 5.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>Show data as a table</summary>
    <table id="signups-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Week</th><th scope="col">Sign-ups</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </details>
</figure>
```

Build the table from the same array you pass to Chart.js, so the two can never disagree:

```js
function fillTable(tableId, labels, values) {
  const tbody = document.querySelector(`#${tableId} tbody`);
  labels.forEach((label, i) => {
    const row = tbody.insertRow();
    row.insertCell().textContent = label;
    row.insertCell().textContent = values[i].toLocaleString();
  });
}

fillTable("signups-table", weeks, signups);
```

`textContent` matters if labels come from an API or a CSV header: string-building with `innerHTML` would let a crafted label inject markup.

A few things the audit may not flag but users will hit:

- Values that exist only in hover tooltips are unreachable by keyboard and screen reader users. Anything important should also be in a direct label, the summary or the table.
- If series differ only by color, add direct labels or distinct point styles so the chart still works for colorblind readers.
- The summary should state the takeaway, not "a line chart of sign-ups". Write it by hand for each chart, or generate it from the data if the charts are dynamic.

With six charts, wrap this in one helper that takes the chart config plus a summary string and outputs the figure, canvas and table, so every chart gets the same treatment.

why does this print 4 4 4 instead of 1 2 3

for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), i * 1000);
}

`var` creates a single `i` for the whole function, not one per iteration. All three callbacks close over that same variable, and by the time they run the loop has finished and `i` is 4.

Change `var` to `let`:

```js
for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), i * 1000);
}
// 1, 2, 3, one per second
```

With `let`, a `for` loop creates a fresh binding of `i` for each iteration, so each callback captures its own value. The delays were already right in your version because `i * 1000` is evaluated immediately; only the callbacks read `i` later.

can you convert this to async/await?

```js
const API_KEY = "sk_live_...";

function getUser(id) {
  return fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${id}`, {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${API_KEY}` },
  })
    .then((res) => res.json())
    .then((data) => data.user)
    .catch((err) => console.error(err));
}
```

```js
const API_KEY = process.env.EXAMPLE_API_KEY;

async function getUser(id) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users/${id}`, {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${API_KEY}` },
  });
  if (!res.ok) {
    throw new Error(`GET /users/${id} failed: ${res.status}`);
  }
  const data = await res.json();
  return data.user;
}
```

I made two behavior changes. I added a check on `res.ok`, because `fetch` only rejects on network errors: a 404 or 500 would otherwise be parsed as if it were a valid user. And I let errors propagate instead of catching them here, since your `.catch` logged the error and then returned `undefined`, which callers couldn't tell apart from a user with no data. Put the `try/catch` where you actually handle the failure.

Your original code has a live secret key hard-coded. If it's a real key, rotate it in the provider's dashboard and read the new one from an environment variable as above. If this code runs in the browser rather than in Node, `process.env` won't exist and no secret key is safe there at all: the request has to go through your backend.

È la *preflight* di CORS. Prima di una richiesta cross-origin "non semplice" il browser chiede al server, con una OPTIONS, se quel metodo e quegli header sono ammessi. Una POST diventa non semplice quando ha `Content-Type: application/json` o un header come `Authorization`, cioè quasi sempre in un'API JSON.

Il tuo server risponde 404 alla OPTIONS perché nessuna route la gestisce, quindi il browser blocca la POST vera e non la manda nemmeno.

Con Express il middleware `cors` gestisce le preflight da solo, purché sia registrato con `app.use` prima delle route:

```js
app.use(cors({ origin: "http://localhost:5173" }));
app.use(express.json());
app.use("/api/ordini", ordiniRouter);
```

Per verificare, nel tab Network del browser la OPTIONS deve rispondere 204 (o 200) con `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` e `Access-Control-Allow-Methods` che include `POST`, e subito dopo deve comparire la POST.

```php
$prefs = isset($_COOKIE['prefs']) ? unserialize($_COOKIE['prefs']) : [];
$theme = $prefs['theme'] ?? 'light';
```
we store UI prefs in a cookie, laravel app. risk?

Yes, this is PHP object injection, and in a Laravel app it is likely exploitable for remote code execution.

`unserialize` on attacker-controlled data can create objects of any class the app can autoload, and their magic methods such as `__destruct` or `__wakeup` run automatically. Laravel and many common Composer packages contain known chains of such classes, and public tools generate ready-made payloads for them. The attacker only has to set the cookie. Whether a chain works depends on your exact dependency versions, which I can't see, so treat it as critical rather than betting on it.

Store preferences as JSON, which only produces arrays and scalars:

```php
$prefs = json_decode($_COOKIE['prefs'] ?? '[]', true);
if (!is_array($prefs)) {
    $prefs = [];
}
$theme = in_array($prefs['theme'] ?? null, ['light', 'dark'], true) ? $prefs['theme'] : 'light';
```

In Laravel you can also use the framework's encrypted cookies (the `EncryptCookies` middleware), so the client can't tamper with the value at all; just make sure this cookie isn't in its `$except` list.

getting error TS18048: 'user' is possibly 'undefined'. on the second line

const user = users.find(u => u.id === id);
console.log(user.name);

`Array.prototype.find` returns `undefined` when nothing matches, so `user` has type `User | undefined`, and strict mode makes you handle the missing case before reading `.name`.

Decide what should happen when the user isn't there:

```ts
const user = users.find((u) => u.id === id);
if (!user) {
  throw new Error(`User ${id} not found`); // or return a 404, a default...
}
console.log(user.name); // user is User from here on
```

If a missing user is fine and you only need the name, optional chaining is shorter:

```ts
console.log(user?.name ?? "unknown");
```

Tutti i `case` di uno `switch` condividono lo stesso blocco, quindi due `const messaggio` al suo interno sono una doppia dichiarazione, anche se in rami diversi. Aggiungi le graffe per dare a ogni `case` il suo blocco:

```js
switch (stato) {
  case 'errore': {
    const messaggio = 'Qualcosa è andato storto';
    mostra(messaggio);
    break;
  }
  case 'ok': {
    const messaggio = 'Salvato';
    mostra(messaggio);
    break;
  }
}
```

In alternativa dichiara `let messaggio` una volta prima dello `switch` e assegnalo nei vari rami.

